AB  - The proliferation of agentic AI is generating an energy consumption crisis that neither incremental hardware improvements nor renewable energy procurement alone can resolve. This survey argues that the most viable pathway toward sustainable autonomous intelligence lies not in making Large Language Models (LLMs) greener, but in fundamentally rethinking the unit of agency: replacing monolithic frontier models with Small Language Models (SLMs) that are purpose-designed for the constrained, repetitive, and specialized task profiles that characterize the vast majority of deployed agentic workloads. A structured review of the small-model landscape collates published evidence that 4-bit quantized models in the 1B–7B range approach LLM-scale performance on several classes of agentic sub-tasks, with energy-per-token reductions of about one order of magnitude reported by individual studies for the specific model pairs, hardware, and workloads they evaluate, while unlocking edge deployment on sub-watt neuromorphic and embedded hardware that frontier models cannot approach. Multiple application domains are surveyed, open research challenges are identified across the agentic stack, and the concept of intelligence-per-watt (IPW) is introduced as the central organizing metric for sustainable autonomous intelligence research. This comprehensive survey provides a conceptual analysis of the literature, utilizing order-of-magnitude estimations based on published data, without conducting original physical measurements.
ER  - 

