AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in high-risk domains where opacity, bias, and instability undermine trust and accountability. Traditional explainability methods, often centered on surface outputs, are insufficient for capturing the reasoning pathways, planning logic, and broader sociotechnical implications of agentic LLMs. We introduce TAXAL (Triadic Alignment for eXplainability in Agentic LLMs), a triadic fusion framework that integrates three complementary dimensions of explainability: cognitive (user understanding), functional (practical utility), and causal (faithful reasoning). TAXAL provides a unified, role-sensitive scaffold for designing, evaluating, and deploying explanations across heterogeneous sociotechnical settings. We situate a broad range of explainability methods—from post-hoc attribution and dialogic interfaces to explanation-aware prompting—within the TAXAL framework, and demonstrate its applicability through cross-domain use cases in law, education, healthcare, and public services. These applications show how explanation strategies must adapt to stakeholder roles, institutional constraints, and decision risks. We further validate TAXAL in the medical domain through a structured empirical study on clinical question answering, showing that the framework supports high-quality, audience-adapted explanations across its cognitive, functional, and causal dimensions. The results confirm that TAXAL improves the differentiation, relevance, and perceived suitability of explanations for distinct stakeholders, while providing a practical basis for trustworthy, contestable, and accountable human–AI collaboration.
