AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in healthcare chatbots; however, they are prone to hallucination and lack reliable domain knowledge without external grounding. This paper presents an empirical study of knowledge modelling approaches for healthcare chatbots, using a corpus of Australian post-stroke driving guidelines. We evaluate the performance of raw LLMs, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and knowledge-graph-based retrieval (GraphRAG). Experimental results show that knowledge augmentation improves accuracy, with GraphRAG achieving the best performance among retrieval-based methods. However, our findings also indicate that neither RAG nor GraphRAG provides a complete solution, highlighting the need for more robust and hybrid strategies to support reliable healthcare chatbot systems.
