KW  - Software architecture
KW  - Multi-agent systems
KW  - Large Language Models
KW  - Project management
KW  - Agentic AI
KW  - Natural language processing
AB  - Context:
The manual translation of unstructured team dialogue into the structured artifacts required for Information Technology (IT) project governance is a critical bottleneck in modern information systems management.
Objectives:
We introduce and validate DevNous, a novel Large Language Model-based (LLM) multi-agent system architecture, to automate this unstructured-to-structured translation process.
Methods:
DevNous integrates directly into team chat environments, identifying actionable intents from informal dialogue and managing stateful, multi-turn workflows for core administrative tasks like automated task formalization and progress summary synthesis. To quantitatively evaluate the architecture, we introduce a new benchmark of 160 realistic, interactive conversational turns. The dataset was manually annotated with a multi-label ground truth and is publicly available.
Results:
On this benchmark, DevNous achieves an exact match turn accuracy of 81.3% and a multiset F1-Score of 0.845, providing strong evidence for its viability. In a controlled comparative experiment, our proposed architecture outperforms a monolithic agent baseline and ablation configurations across multiple models.
Conclusion:
The primary contributions of this work are threefold: (1) a validated ambient, hierarchical architectural pattern for AI-native administrative agents, (2) the first empirical baseline and public benchmark dataset for this challenging problem domain, and (3) architectural design insights via ablations, error taxonomy, and operational metrics.
ER  - 

