AB  - Mainstream LLM chat tools converge on a linear conversation model, but users routinely consult several models on the same question, switch between specialists within a single task, run parallel analytical branches for comparison, and return to earlier turns to explore alternatives—work that is tree-shaped, not list-shaped. Multi-provider access, mid-thread model switching, and cross-provider tool integration via the Model Context Protocol (MCP) are frontier features that several existing platforms already offer. We present AIbranch, an open-source platform that pairs these features with a first-class visible conversation tree: every fork and every model handoff appears as a navigable node in a single workspace, so the branch structure and the model choices that shaped each turn remain visible instead of being hidden behind per-turn menus. The architecture is designed for reusability and modularity, with a hub-plus-engines decomposition. The code base is released under the Apache License 2.0.
