AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a promising paradigm for improving factual grounding in large language models (LLMs), yet its effectiveness in low-resource, morphologically rich languages remains underexplored. This paper presents RAG-C, a RAG framework tailored for Amharic legal question answering (QA), integrating semantic chunking, multilingual contextual embeddings, and efficient vector retrieval. We construct an 82.4 MB Amharic corpus from publicly available Ethiopian Federal Supreme Court cassation decisions (legal precedents), complemented by Amharic Wikipedia and news sources. We evaluate across 500 QA pairs using both automatic metrics (RAGAS, F1, BLEU, EM) and human judgment. RAG-C achieves state-of-the-art performance across all evaluation dimensions, including 0.797 context relevance, 0.833 faithfulness, and 0.772 F1, outperforming strong baselines such as BM25 and zero-shot LLMs. Human evaluation further confirms improvements in factual correctness (4.5/5) and overall quality (4.4/5). Our analysis shows that embedding quality is the dominant factor in retrieval performance, while semantic chunking improves contextual coherence in morphologically complex text. We also identify key trade-offs in retrieval depth, model scaling, and latency, and highlight limitations related to corpus coverage and statistical testing. These results demonstrate that carefully designed RAG pipelines can substantially improve QA performance in low-resource languages, providing a scalable path toward reliable, grounded NLP systems beyond high-resource settings.
ER  - 

