AB  - With the exponential growth of information in the Internet era, Recommender Systems play a pivotal role in helping users discover content of interest. However, traditional recommendation paradigms rely heavily on large-scale human-annotated data and still face limitations in expressive power and content generation when dealing with semantically rich and context-sensitive recommendation tasks due to the intricate feature engineering. To overcome these challenges, generative models, owing to their powerful semantic modeling and content generation capabilities, have been integrated into recommender systems, leading to the emergence of Generative Recommender Systems (GRS). GRS can better understand user preferences and generate diverse and highly personalized recommendation content, significantly enhancing user experience and recommendation effectiveness. This paper presents a comprehensive and in-depth survey of GRS. Distinct from previous surveys, our work provides comprehensive coverage of both Large Language Models (LLMs)-driven recommendation paradigms and applications of other mainstream generative models in recommendation tasks. In addition, we introduce a novel perspective to systematically categorize the key technical paths of GRS, exploring current research advances across multiple dimensions, including data representation, model architecture, information fusion, and evaluation methodologies. Furthermore, this paper summarizes the critical engineering challenges and practical approaches encountered during the industrial deployment of GRS, discussing their technical advantages and limitations in typical industrial scenarios. Finally, we provide insight into future research directions in this field, offering a more comprehensive perspective and a deeper understanding to relevant researchers and practitioners.
