AB  - Large language models (LLMs) make natural-language interaction with enterprise systems practical, but interpreting a request and changing organizational state remain distinct problems. We present a process-bound Governed Intent-to-Execution Architecture centered on an Intent Execution Contract (IEC) that binds interpreted intent to a registered process, structured arguments, preconditions, authorization, confirmation policy, permitted tools, side-effect class, recovery behavior, and execution provenance. Kooshi is a working reference implementation: the LLM interprets intent and extracts grounded values, while process definitions determine required information, validation, state transitions, confirmation, and tool scope. An author-maintained implementation-conformance tracker records 210/210 passes across seven operational scenarios, although the available tracker lacks per-run execution traces. To compare architectures, we developed a publicly accessible .NET benchmark of ProcessBound, WorkflowAgent, and DirectLlm. In a one-repetition real-LLM v2 pilot, 18/21 scheduled scenarios satisfied predefined outcomes: 7/7, 6/7, and 5/7, respectively. Of three remaining cases, one was an observed DirectLlm criterion violation; two were unstarted because a provider-usage accounting gate stopped the runner. For the five booking-producing scenarios per architecture, correctly completed bookings were 5/5, 5/5, and 4/5, respectively. A separate 21-case simulated benchmark passed in public CI with Docker. These observations demonstrate feasibility and reproducible measurement, not statistical superiority or production reliability. The contribution is a concrete ingress architecture linking human intent to governed process execution, with distinct implementation and comparative evidence.
