KW  - Software testing
KW  - NLP software
KW  - LLMs
KW  - Best-first search
AB  - Context:
With the breakthrough advancements in Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI), Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive performance in Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks. LLM-based NLP software is rapidly gaining traction across various domains, including financial analysis and content moderation. However, these applications frequently exhibit robustness deficiencies, where subtle perturbations in the input (prompt + example) may lead to erroneous outputs. Current robustness testing methods face two main limitations: (1) low testing effectiveness, limiting the applicability of LLM-based software in safety–critical scenarios, and (2) insufficient naturalness of test cases, reducing the practical value of testing outcomes.
Objective:
To this end, this paper treats the prompt and example as a unified user input. We propose ABFS, a simple yet effective automated robustness testing method for LLM-based NLP software.
Method:
ABFS formulates testing process as a combinatorial optimization problem over a high-dimensional perturbation space, employing Best-First Search to execute perturbations by maintaining a priority queue that ranks globally promising perturbations, thereby enhancing testing effectiveness. In addition, we propose a novel Adaptive control strategy that dynamically updates word importance based on accumulated historical feedback, thus generating reasonable and natural test cases.
Results:
We evaluate the robustness testing performance of ABFS on three datasets across five threat models. On Llama2-13b, the traditional StressTest achieves a success rate of only 13.273%, while ABFS attains a success rate of 98.064%. Compared to baseline methods, ABFS introduces fewer modifications to the original input and consistently generates test cases with superior naturalness. Furthermore, test cases generated by ABFS exhibit stronger transferability and higher testing efficiency, significantly reducing testing costs.
Conclusion:
These findings indicate that ABFS can generate efficient and natural test cases, revealing robustness flaws in LLM-based NLP software. Compared to existing methods, ABFS enables more comprehensive and realistic pre-deployment testing.
