AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances large language models (LLMs) with external knowledge but raises privacy concerns when using sensitive data. We propose Sampled and Privately Aggregated Retrieval-Augmented Generation (SPARAG), a privacy-preserving RAG framework that combines database-level differential privacy in retrieval with randomized token sanitization during generation. By perturbing document scoring and applying randomized token replacement to generated outputs, SPARAG mitigates information leakage while retaining utility. Experiments on SQuAD, AGNews, TriviaQA, and the challenging long-context multimodal LoCoMo dataset show that SPARAG achieves strong privacy protection with minimal utility loss, outperforming existing baselines in the utility–privacy trade-off. The tunable privacy parameters make SPARAG practical for secure deployment of RAG in sensitive, knowledge-intensive domains.
