AB  - This survey investigates the dual impact of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) on cybersecurity by analyzing over two hundred studies published between 2020 and 2025. Our findings show that GAI acts both as a defense enabler by automating anomaly detection, accelerating incident response, and simulating attacks for system hardening, and as a threat vector powering advanced phishing, deepfake impersonation, model poisoning, and adversarial exploits. Based on this analysis, we propose a structured taxonomy with three core dimensions: GAI for cyber defense, GAI as a source of threats, and mitigation strategies. Unlike prior surveys, this taxonomy highlights research asymmetries: while phishing detection and synthetic media threats are extensively studied, defensive strategies against adversarial poisoning or large-scale model exploitation remain underexplored. We further identify that malicious uses of GAI are evolving more rapidly than defensive countermeasures, creating a critical gap in cybersecurity readiness. These insights underscore that GAI is not simply a technological advancement but a disruptive force reshaping both offensive and defensive practices, and they call for targeted research efforts to address uncovered areas.
