AB  - The proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices has intensified the demand for intelligent on-device natural language processing. Although large language models (LLMs) have significantly advanced natural language processing, their computational and memory requirements render them unsuitable for most resource-constrained IoT environments. Small language models (SLMs), typically defined as models with fewer than 7 billion parameters, offer a viable alternative by enabling edge intelligence without relying on cloud infrastructure. Previous surveys have often addressed model architectures, optimization strategies, and hardware constraints separately. In contrast, this survey offers an integrated analysis that, to the best of our knowledge, jointly addresses these aspects with a focus on real-world IoT deployments. A structured review of over 160 papers (2019–2026) was conducted to synthesize various insights across architectures, optimization techniques, hardware compatibility, and real-world representative IoT deployments. Key findings show that SLMs deliver competitive accuracy in classification and narrow-domain tasks, with multi-step reasoning requiring models with 1.5–3 B parameters while offering clear advantages in privacy, latency, energy efficiency, and offline operation. Open challenges and future directions for SLM-enabled IoT systems are also discussed.
