AB  - Large language model (LLM) agent systems convert model reasoning into consequential actions such as tool calls, database updates and publication. Existing controls can authorise actions, constrain execution paths and evaluate provenance or relational state. However, a transition may remain structurally inadmissible because of policy-relevant state in the derivation that produced its artefact. We formalise derivation-relative structural admissibility and operationalise it through the Pre-Execution Governance Framework (PEGF), a deterministic architecture that validates dependencies, reconstructs derivation ancestry and evaluates actions before effects occur. Using publication as an evaluated terminal instantiation, PEGF revalidates the consumed draft's claim–evidence relation against its evidence ancestry and, under an exact-equality policy, requires the relation to preserve that baseline. Across 27 matched configurations, relation-preserving cases were allowed, while all relation-substitution cases were denied with E.ADM.CLAIM_DRIFT after publication-binding checks succeeded. FULL_STAGE2 denied 77/77 structural adversarial traces and preserved 25/25 policy-admissible workflows. On AgentDojo-derived Transfer-B traces, PEGF denied 80/80 adversarial traces and preserved 40/40 benign traces, compared with 16/80 denials by a sequence-only finite-state machine. Across 65 benign traces, PEGF added median control processing of 3.88 ms and 2.22% paired replay overhead. These results support derivation-relative structural control without establishing semantic truth, provenance truthfulness or end-to-end deployment guarantees.
ER  - 

