AB  - Tool-using large language model (LLM) agents can compound early errors across long trajectories. Existing safeguards mainly verify outcomes after execution or store unvalidated reflections; they do not provide an auditable pre-action policy that jointly controls risk, irreversibility, and cost. We introduce the Meta-Reasoning Insight Framework (MRIF), whose novelty is a verifiable action-level contract linking heterogeneous uncertainty estimates to complexity-aware routing, verifier-gated reflection, and a provenance-bearing knowledge graph. Structural risk uses logistic interactions, mixture weights use projected Brier updates, and graph assertions become retrievable only after schema, trace-evidence, provenance, and verifier checks. Evaluation covers complete ToolTalk (78 conversations, 266 calls), BFCL V4 transfer (2,501 tasks, 13,208 derived action records), and disjoint 100-task BFCL studies. On held-out ToolTalk, MRIF obtains a Brier score of 0.141, ECE of 0.126, AUROC of 0.833, and AUPRC of 0.949. On the BFCL holdout, frozen MRIF-v2 reaches 69% success versus 64–68% for four official-code-informed adaptations, although paired advantages are not significant and MRIF costs more. Across three OpenAI base models, success varies from 68% to 75%. MRIF therefore supplies auditable, risk-gated meta-reasoning while exposing calibration, efficiency, and base-model-dependence boundaries relevant to responsible deployment.
ER  - 

