AB  - Dongba script is recognized as the world’s only living hieroglyphic system, and the ancient manuscripts written in this script have been inscribed on the UNESCO’s Memory of the World Register. However, the automatic translation of Dongba manuscripts presents significant challenges due to their complex semantics characterized by polysemy, non-linear spatial layouts, and weakly grammatical structures. To address these challenges, we propose a novel Glyph-Coordinate Fusion (GC-Fusion) strategy. By explicitly injecting the associated information of glyph encodings and spatial coordinates for individual characters into prompts, we constructed a multimodal GC-Fusion Instruction Dataset for the instruction fine-tuning of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). This approach guides the model to focus on specific visual regions and their semantic associations, thereby achieving accurate translation of Dongba manuscripts. We conducted comprehensive evaluations on multiple state-of-the-art MLLMs. Experimental results demonstrate that our strategy significantly outperforms Visual-Only baselines. Notably, under full-parameter fine-tuning, Qwen2.5-VL achieves outstanding performance with a BLEU-4 score of 25.36 and a BERTScore of 42.89. This work provides a validated framework for MLLM-based Dongba manuscript translation under manually verified glyph-coordinate guidance.
ER  - 

