AB  - With the widespread application of deep neural networks (DNNs) and the increasing deployment of large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs) in security-sensitive domains, adversarial robustness has become a critical concern. Adversarial examples are inputs specifically crafted by adding imperceptible perturbations to a legitimate example, which can easily mislead a model into producing incorrect predictions. Existing detection methods often rely on extracting features from all layers, leading to high computational costs and limited scalability. To address this, we propose NeuronSig, a lightweight adversarial example detection framework based on single-layer neuron activation patterns. We introduce the concept of a neuron signature, which combines the activation magnitudes and their binary activation properties to capture the distinguishing patterns of adversarial inputs. This enables efficient and generalizable detection without modifying the target model. NeuronSig is evaluated across five datasets and nine adversarial attack types, consistently achieving an AUC of around 0.95 and maintaining robustness against adaptive attacks. We further discuss the potential applicability of NeuronSig to Transformer-based architectures, including LLMs and VLMs, through preliminary exploratory observations. While the current study focuses on CNN-based models, these observations suggest that neuron activation signatures may provide a useful direction for future adversarial detection research in modern foundation models.
