AB  - As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in real-world scenarios, their trustworthiness has emerged as a critical bottleneck hindering reliable adoption. However, current evaluation methods predominantly rely on static benchmarks, which fail to capture the continuous and strategic interactions between human and LLM, which leads to a “reliability illusion” in practical applications. We propose a Multi-stressor Multi-dimensional Dynamic Evaluation framework (M2DE) to address the problem. First, based on initial seed datasets of stressor, it generates testing prompts via the Free-Exploration and Scripted-Execution Mode, guaranteeing the diversity and complementarity. Second, it introduces a closed-loop Creator-Verifier-Evaluator multi-agent architecture to deeply explore the behaviors of LLMs by adaptive multi-turn interactions. Finally, it quantifies the trustworthiness of models across five dimensions with three key metrics. Experimental results uncover multiple vulnerabilities overlooked by the static and simple dynamic evaluations, and demonstrate the remarkable advantage of M2DE. These elaborate evaluations can further empower LLM developers for targeted model debugging and optimization.
