AB  - Artificial intelligence is rapidly transforming medical imaging, particularly through the integration of large language models, especially large multimodal models that process both text and imaging data. A new paradigm known as agentic AI uses embedded LLMs within software systems to observe, reason, and act autonomously over time. These systems often operate within iterative control loops that enable decision-making, tool use, and interaction with external data sources. An extension of this concept is the multi-agent system (MAS), in which multiple specialized agents collaborate to solve complex tasks through communication and coordination. These architectures are particularly relevant to radiology, where workflows involve multiple stages, including imaging acquisition, report generation, clinical decision support, and quality assurance. However, despite growing interest and rapid technical progress, most agentic AI systems, including multi-agent architectures, in healthcare remain research-use-only technologies that have not yet undergone sufficient prospective validation or regulatory approval for widespread autonomous clinical deployment. This article reviews the current technological landscape of agentic and multi-agent architectures, describes the underlying system components and orchestration patterns, and evaluates the major frameworks for building such systems. We further discuss emerging interoperability standards and infrastructure for observability and reliability, and consider how these architectures may enable future clinical decision support systems and automated imaging workflows.
