AB  - The entire ecosystem of cybersecurity is experiencing a profound change due to the augmentation of Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning (ML), and Generative AI (GenAI) techniques. The major drawback of conventional rule-based security techniques is that they cannot cope with complex, flexible or adaptable, and extensive threats. Expanding upon these foundations, Generative AI (GenAI) adds a novel dimension of capability—facilitating the fabrication of synthetic data, simulation of attack scenarios, intelligent deception, and the production of dynamic defensive material. This paper examines the defensive applications of GenAI, encompassing synthetic data generation, adversarial simulation, threat intelligence summarization, deception techniques (such as honeypots and honeytokens), automated incident response playbooks, and dynamic rule development. It critically examines the offensive threats associated with GenAI’s dual-use characteristics, including AI-generated phishing, polymorphic malware, and deepfake impersonation. The discussion encompasses the role of GenAI in enhancing Security Operations Center (SOC) training and operations, as well as strategic problems such as scalability, interpretability, and governance. GenAI is not just a new piece of technology; it’s also a big step toward proactive, adaptive, and intelligent cybersecurity frameworks—as long as it’s used with caution, control, and resilience.
