AB  - The widespread adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) poses significant societal risks, necessitating robust and interpretable methods for detecting LLM-generated text. Our approach is predicated on a key observation: LLMs exhibit markedly lower semantic variance when rewriting their own outputs compared to human-written texts, a phenomenon we attribute to the inherent consistency of their generative processes. Capitalizing on this disparity, we introduce Rewriting Iterative Semantic Difference (REISD), a novel detection method. REISD employs a unique feature, the Iterative Semantic Difference (ISD), which quantifies semantic change throughout an iterative rewriting process. Specifically, the ISD is calculated as the difference between the semantic shift of a given text and a baseline derived from LLM self-rewriting. This differential effectively isolates the divergence between a text’s provenance and the model’s intrinsic generative characteristics. Extensive empirical evaluations demonstrate that REISD consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods, exhibiting superior robustness and cross-domain generalization.
