AB  - Text adversarial attacks have brought severe safety challenges to various Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, so designing effective text defense methods has become an urgent research direction. Existing text defense strategies can be roughly categorized into model-driven defense, data-driven defense, and combined defense. Although the combined defense takes advantage of the previous two groups, its continuous perturbation optimization and the random perturbation localization strategies do not match real attackers. Most of the real attack methods directly manipulate discrete text according to different word importance rules. To alleviate these problems, we propose a novel text adversarial defense method, named Discrete Multi-head Training with Rule-based Perturbation Location (DMT-RPL). Firstly, the Discrete Multi-head Training (DMT) simultaneously trains the victim model head and a Masked Language Model (MLM) head to generate discrete adversarial samples, which are closer to real adversarial attacks. The MLM head trained with these high-quality adversarial samples can better recover the adversarial text to clean text. Secondly, the Rule-based Perturbation Location (RPL) integrates the word importance and word frequency rules to accurately locate the perturbations instead of random selection. Accurate perturbation localization is significant in improving text purification and the final defense rate. We assess the defense performance of the proposed DMT-RPL on both text classification tasks and text generation tasks with a total of 25 defense cases. The experiments show DMT-RPL achieves competitive performance against five mainstream baselines, yielding consistent gains in dynamic defense (17.56% improvement), perturbation localization accuracy (11.64% improvement), and transferability under most settings.
